# **1. Executive Summary**

Private AI Ops is a managed AI operations firm. It builds, configures, manages, and continuously improves AI-powered workflows for small and medium-sized businesses that lack the internal technical talent to implement them safely. For privacy-sensitive, regulated, or confidentiality-dependent clients — law firms, dental practices, financial SMBs — it offers optional private AI infrastructure: dedicated GPU inference endpoints, zero-retention architecture, RAG pipelines, HIPAA-ready controls, and client-specific audit logging.

 

| **STRATEGIC VERDICT** |
| --- |
| *Launch as Managed AI Ops Services. Use private AI infrastructure as a premium differentiator, not the headline product. Validate paid workflow demand first; deploy hardware only when pre-sold or when it materially improves close rate. South Florida law firms are the recommended first beachhead.* |

 

## **Summary Parameters**

| **Parameter** | **Position** |
| --- | --- |
| **What the business is** | A managed AI operations firm that builds, manages, and improves AI-powered workflows for privacy-sensitive SMBs. |
| **Core problem solved** | SMBs lack the staff, systems, and workflow discipline to adopt AI safely. Regulated SMBs cannot use public AI tools for confidential data. |
| **Who it serves** | Law firms, dental practices, property managers, and financial SMBs — initially in South Florida. |
| **Why now** | The Heppner ruling (Feb 2026) created an urgent privilege-protection crisis for law firms using public AI. HIPAA exposure from public AI tools is unresolved. The 12–18 month competitive window before large MSPs productize this layer is open. |
| **GPU infrastructure role** | Premium differentiator and long-term moat — not the initial product. Used as a compliance-packaged private AI capability for clients who cannot use public AI tools. |
| **Services-first rationale** | Lower CapEx risk. Validates buyer personas and workflows before hardware commitment. Infrastructure is demand-triggered — deployed only when signed contracts exist. |
| **Launch strategy** | Two verticals simultaneously: law firms (primary) and dental practices (secondary). Founder-led, relationship-driven, local market first. |
| **90-day revenue target** | 2–3 onboarding clients ($7,000–15,000 setup) converting to $3,000–6,000/month combined retainer MRR. |

 

## **Primary Risks**

| **Top Risk** | **Mitigation** |
| --- | --- |
| Fuzzy positioning — sounds like GPU hosting + AI agency + SaaS | Lead every narrative with managed AI Ops outcomes. Infrastructure is a capability, not the pitch. |
| Premature hardware spend before paid demand | Hardware is demand-triggered. No purchase before signed retainer contracts justify it. |
| Compliance overclaiming — creating liability | Use careful language: 'HIPAA-ready,' 'BAA-supported,' 'zero-retention architecture.' Attorney review required before first regulated client. |
| Too many verticals, too fast | Two verticals max in Year 1. Law first. Dental second. Marine is experimental. |
| Founder bandwidth bottleneck at 4+ clients | Hire delivery support at Month 3–4 when retainer count reaches 3–4. |

 

# **2. Unified Business Thesis**

| *SMBs want AI productivity. Most cannot implement AI safely. Regulated SMBs cannot use public AI tools for confidential data. Private AI Ops solves both problems: managed workflow execution with optional private AI infrastructure.* |
| --- |

 

## **Why This Model Is Stronger Than Its Components**

| **Model** | **What It Gets Right** | **What It Misses** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Pure AI consulting** | Deep workflow expertise. High trust. | Does not manage after launch. No recurring revenue. Clients drift. No retention mechanism. |
| **Generic automation agency** | Broad applicability. | No vertical depth. Cannot credibly demo domain workflows. Commoditized. |
| **Raw GPU hosting** | Infrastructure credibility. | Commodity pricing. No managed service moat. SMBs do not buy GPU hours. |
| **Vertical SaaS at launch** | Scalable if market is large enough. | Requires capital, product team, and a market that self-serves. Not the near-term profile. |
| **Private AI Ops (combined)** | Recurring services revenue. Vertical depth. Infrastructure moat. Trust-based SMB sales. Private compliance capability. | Requires active sales and delivery. Not passive. Scales through people and playbooks before platform. |

 

## **The Strategic Gap Being Filled**

Anthropic and OpenAI have built powerful general-purpose AI. Enterprise consulting firms have built AI transformation practices for large companies. Neither has built the last-mile layer for SMBs in regulated or operationally complex niches. MSPs manage devices, email, and security — not AI workflows. No firm is showing up at a 6-attorney law firm in Fort Lauderdale and saying: 'We will configure your AI intake workflows, maintain them monthly, and run them on private infrastructure so your client data never reaches a public AI server.'

That gap is the business. The competitive window is 12–18 months before large MSPs productize this layer. Speed to first retainer client in each vertical is the only metric that matters in Year 1.

 

