## **Law Firms — Detailed Analysis**

Law firms are the strongest beachhead for the combined strategy. The Heppner ruling (S.D.N.Y., February 2026) found that attorney-client privilege was waived when documents were created using publicly available AI platforms because data was voluntarily disclosed to a third-party server. This is an urgent, real, and legally actionable pain point — not a theoretical risk.

| **Dimension** | **Detail** |
| --- | --- |
| **Primary buyer** | Managing partner or practice administrator at a 3–15 attorney firm |
| **Core pain** | Privilege exposure from public AI. High volume of repetitive document work. Admin burden on attorneys doing non-billable tasks. |
| **Automatable workflows** | Client intake summaries, discovery document organization, deposition prep summaries, demand letter drafts, case chronology, billing narrative cleanup, research memos, matter status summaries, document comparison, deadline extraction. |
| **Sales angle** | Lead with Heppner. Frame private endpoint + DPA as privilege preservation. Price as half of OpenAI Enterprise that still exposes their data. |
| **Objection — trust** | Founder-led local relationship selling. Bar association involvement. Case study from first client. |
| **Retention driver** | After 90 days, the firm's intake SOP, billing templates, and research workflows are built around the Private AI Ops system. Switching cost is very high. |

 

## **Marine / Charter — Special Assessment**

Charter Brief is a proven product concept and a useful community distribution asset. However, marine charter operators are a weaker beachhead for managed AI retainer business. The market is small (~5,000 operators), privacy sensitivity is low (no regulatory urgency), willingness to pay for a $1,500/month retainer is lower, and the FareHarbor automation workflow, while compelling, is narrower than the law or dental use cases. Recommendation: Keep Charter Brief as a free distribution tool and community asset. Do not hire a dedicated marine vertical lead in Year 1. If marine operators convert to retainer at better-than-expected rates, evaluate a Part-Time marine lead in Year 2.

 

