## **Outreach Message — Law Firm (Email)**

| *Subject: The Heppner ruling and your firm**'**s AI usage  Hi [Name],  A federal judge ruled in February that using publicly available AI platforms can waive attorney-client privilege because data is disclosed to a third-party server. If your firm is using ChatGPT or consumer-grade AI tools for client work, you have exposure.  I help law firms in South Florida implement AI workflows on private infrastructure — your data never reaches a public AI server. Clients are automating intake summaries, billing narratives, and matter status updates at a fraction of their current time investment.  Worth 20 minutes to see if it applies to your practice?  [Name]* |
| --- |

 

## **Discovery Call Script**

| **Step** | **Action** |
| --- | --- |
| **Open** | Run a live demo: take a real intake email or document they hand you and run it through a private workflow. Let them react before explaining anything. |
| **Pain inventory** | 'Walk me through a typical week for your paralegals and admins. What takes the most time that feels like it shouldn't require a lawyer or a paralegal?' |
| **Map and quantify** | List every workflow named. Estimate hours per week. Multiply by paralegal rate ($25–40/hr) or attorney rate if they're doing it themselves. Show them the monthly dollar figure before presenting any solution. |
| **Introduce concept** | 'We configure and manage AI workflows for law firms — and for firms with confidentiality concerns, we run them on private infrastructure with a signed Data Processing Agreement. Our clients typically recover our fee within 60 days in paralegal time alone.' |
| **Scope on call** | Name which workflows, which integrations, whether private infrastructure is needed. Name a price range before the call ends. Do not leave without a number. |
| **Close** | 'If this makes sense, I'll send a one-page scope this afternoon and we can start the workflow audit next week.' A start date, not a follow-up. |

 

